[32m+[m[32m# Deployment[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mHow to run the full stack (Postgres + `ml-service` + `java-backend`) on a single always-on VM,[m
[32m+[m[32mbehind an nginx reverse proxy with HTTPS.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThis guide targets the **Oracle Cloud Infrastructure (OCI) Always Free** tier because it gives you a[m
[32m+[m[32mgenuinely always-on machine with enough RAM to run all three containers plus a[m
[32m+[m[32m`sentence-transformers` model. Nothing here is OCI-specific except the "Provision the VM" and[m
[32m+[m[32m"Cloud-side firewall" sections — the rest applies to any Ubuntu host.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 0. What you'll end up with[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m[32m                    Internet[m
[32m+[m[32m                       │  :80 / :443[m
[32m+[m[32m                       ▼[m
[32m+[m[32m             ┌───────────────────┐[m
[32m+[m[32m             │  nginx (host)     │   TLS termination, Let's Encrypt cert[m
[32m+[m[32m             └─────────┬─────────┘[m
[32m+[m[32m                       │  proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080[m
[32m+[m[32m                       ▼[m
[32m+[m[32m   ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐[m
[32m+[m[32m   │  docker compose  (bridge network)             │[m
[32m+[m[32m   │                                               │[m
[32m+[m[32m   │   java-backend ──► ml-service (ml-service:8000)│[m
[32m+[m[32m   │        │                                      │[m
[32m+[m[32m   │        ▼                                      │[m
[32m+[m[32m   │     postgres  (pantry_pg_data volume)         │[m
[32m+[m[32m   └───────────────────────────────────────────────┘[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- Only nginx is exposed to the internet (ports 80/443). Postgres and `ml-service` are never[m
[32m+[m[32m  published on a host port; `java-backend` is published only on `127.0.0.1:8080` (loopback), so the[m
[32m+[m[32m  only way in from outside is through nginx.[m
[32m+[m[32m- The three containers talk to each other over the compose bridge network by service name[m
[32m+[m[32m  (`postgres`, `ml-service`, `java-backend`).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 1. Provision the VM (Oracle Cloud Always Free)[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. Create an OCI account and sign in to the Console.[m
[32m+[m[32m2. **Compute → Instances → Create instance.**[m
[32m+[m[32m   - **Image:** Canonical Ubuntu 24.04 (or 22.04).[m
[32m+[m[32m   - **Shape:** `VM.Standard.A1.Flex` (Ampere / ARM). The Always Free allowance is up to 4 OCPU and[m
[32m+[m[32m     24 GB RAM across all your A1 instances — 2 OCPU / 12 GB is plenty for this stack and leaves[m
[32m+[m[32m     headroom. (The older `VM.Standard.E2.1.Micro` AMD micro shape is also Always Free but its 1 GB[m
[32m+[m[32m     RAM is too small once the embedding model loads.)[m
[32m+[m[32m   - **SSH keys:** upload your public key — you'll need the matching private key to connect.[m
[32m+[m[32m   - **Networking:** let it create a new VCN and subnet, and make sure the instance gets a **public[m
[32m+[m[32m     IPv4 address**.[m
[32m+[m[32m3. Note the instance's **public IP** once it boots.[m
[32m+[m[32m4. Point a DNS **A record** for your domain (e.g. `pantry.example.com`) at that public IP. HTTPS[m
[32m+[m[32m   issuance in step 7 needs the name to resolve before you start.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m> **ARM note:** the A1 shape is `aarch64`. All four base images used here[m
[32m+[m[32m> (`postgres:16`, `python:3.12-slim`, `maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-21`, `eclipse-temurin:21-jre-jammy`)[m
[32m+[m[32m> are multi-arch and pull an arm64 variant automatically, and the CPU-only PyTorch wheel the[m
[32m+[m[32m> `ml-service` image installs has an `aarch64` build. Because the images are **built on the VM[m
[32m+[m[32m> itself** (`docker compose build`), architecture takes care of itself — you don't need buildx or[m
[32m+[m[32m> cross-compilation. Building `java-backend` downloads the full Maven dependency set the first time[m
[32m+[m[32m> and the `ml-service` build downloads PyTorch (~200 MB) plus bakes the ~80 MB embedding model, so[m
[32m+[m[32m> the first `docker compose build` on a fresh VM takes a while; subsequent builds reuse the layer[m
[32m+[m[32m> cache.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 2. Connect and update[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mssh ubuntu@<public-ip>[m
[32m+[m[32msudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get -y upgrade[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 3. Install Docker Engine + Compose plugin[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mUse Docker's official apt repository (the `docker.io` package in Ubuntu's own repo is older and[m
[32m+[m[32mships Compose v1):[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32msudo apt-get -y install ca-certificates curl[m
[32m+[m[32msudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings[m
[32m+[m[32msudo curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc[m
[32m+[m[32msudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mecho \[m
[32m+[m[32m  "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc] \[m
[32m+[m[32m  https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu $(. /etc/os-release && echo "$VERSION_CODENAME") stable" \[m
[32m+[m[32m  | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32msudo apt-get update[m
[32m+[m[32msudo apt-get -y install docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m# run docker without sudo (log out / back in afterwards for it to take effect)[m
[32m+[m[32msudo usermod -aG docker $USER[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mVerify:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mdocker version[m
[32m+[m[32mdocker compose version      # expect v2.x ("compose plugin"), invoked as `docker compose`[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m> This repo's files say `docker-compose` (the v1 hyphenated binary) in a few places for historical[m
[32m+[m[32m> reasons. On the VM use `docker compose` (space). The compose file itself is compatible with both.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 4. Get the code and configure secrets[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32msudo apt-get -y install git[m
[32m+[m[32mgit clone <your-repo-url> meal-grocery-assistant[m
[32m+[m[32mcd meal-grocery-assistant[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mcp .env.example .env[m
[32m+[m[32mnano .env        # fill in real values — see below[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m`.env` must contain (see `.env.example` for the annotated template):[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m| Variable        | What it is                                                                 |[m
[32m+[m[32m|-----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|[m
[32m+[m[32m| `OCR_API_KEY`   | Gemini API key — receipt OCR, price-tag OCR, fallback nutrition estimate. |[m
[32m+[m[32m| `USDA_API_KEY`  | USDA FoodData Central key (`DEMO_KEY` works for light testing only).      |[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m`.env` is gitignored — it never gets committed. `docker compose` reads it automatically from the[m
[32m+[m[32mproject directory for `${VAR}` substitution in `docker-compose.yml`, and passes `OCR_API_KEY` /[m
[32m+[m[32m`USDA_API_KEY` through to the `java-backend` container as environment variables. The compose file[m
[32m+[m[32muses `${OCR_API_KEY:?...}` / `${USDA_API_KEY:?...}`, so `docker compose up` fails fast with a clear[m
[32m+[m[32mmessage if either is missing or empty.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mDatabase credentials are **not** in `.env` — Postgres runs only on the internal compose network with[m
[32m+[m[32mthe default `postgres` / `postgres` / `pantrydb` values baked into `docker-compose.yml`, and is[m
[32m+[m[32mnever reachable from outside the host. If you want to change them, edit the `postgres` service[m
[32m+[m[32menvironment and the `SPRING_DATASOURCE_*` values in `docker-compose.yml` together.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Optional: recipe dataset[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mRecipe search and meal-plan generation need `java-backend/src/main/resources/data/recipes.csv`[m
[32m+[m[32m(see `RecipeDataLoader.java` for the column format). It's loaded into the DB once on the first[m
[32m+[m[32mbackend startup. If you add it after the stack is already running, restart the backend:[m
[32m+[m[32m`docker compose restart java-backend`.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 5. How the services are published[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m`docker-compose.yml` is already set up for this:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- **`java-backend`** publishes `127.0.0.1:8080:8080` — loopback only. `curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/inventory`[m
[32m+[m[32m  works **on the VM** (and that's what nginx proxies to), but nothing on `8080` is reachable from[m
[32m+[m[32m  the internet even before the firewall rules below.[m
[32m+[m[32m- **`ml-service`** uses `expose` (no host port) — reachable only from `java-backend` over the[m
[32m+[m[32m  compose network.[m
[32m+[m[32m- **`postgres`** publishes `5432:5432`. That is convenient for connecting a DB client from your[m
[32m+[m[32m  laptop over an SSH tunnel, but if you don't need that, drop the `ports:` block from the[m
[32m+[m[32m  `postgres` service so it's internal-only like `ml-service`. Either way the firewall below never[m
[32m+[m[32m  opens 5432 to the internet.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 6. Firewall[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mTwo layers have to agree: OCI's virtual firewall (security list / NSG) **and** the iptables rules[m
[32m+[m[32minside the Ubuntu instance. Oracle's Ubuntu image ships with a restrictive `INPUT` chain that[m
[32m+[m[32malready blocks most inbound traffic, so you must open ports in *both* places.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### 6a. Cloud-side (OCI Console)[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Networking → Virtual Cloud Networks → your VCN → Security Lists → Default Security List →[m
[32m+[m[32mAdd Ingress Rules:**[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m| Source CIDR | Protocol | Dest. port | Purpose |[m
[32m+[m[32m|-------------|----------|-----------|---------|[m
[32m+[m[32m| `0.0.0.0/0` | TCP      | 80        | HTTP (redirects to HTTPS + ACME challenge) |[m
[32m+[m[32m| `0.0.0.0/0` | TCP      | 443       | HTTPS |[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mPort 22 (SSH) is already allowed by the default rule. **Do not** add ingress rules for 8080, 8000,[m
[32m+[m[32mor 5432 — those stay internal.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### 6b. Instance-side (iptables)[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mOracle's Ubuntu image has an `INPUT` chain ending in a `REJECT` rule; new `ACCEPT` rules must be[m
[32m+[m[32minserted *before* it. Insert at an explicit position (the SSH rule is usually rule 5 or 6 — check[m
[32m+[m[32mwith `sudo iptables -L INPUT --line-numbers`):[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32msudo iptables -I INPUT 6 -m state --state NEW -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT[m
[32m+[m[32msudo iptables -I INPUT 7 -m state --state NEW -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m# persist across reboots[m
[32m+[m[32msudo apt-get -y install netfilter-persistent iptables-persistent[m
[32m+[m[32msudo netfilter-persistent save[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mIf you prefer `ufw`, first make sure its rules land ahead of Oracle's REJECT (they normally do once[m
[32m+[m[32m`ufw` is enabled): `sudo ufw allow OpenSSH && sudo ufw allow 80 && sudo ufw allow 443 && sudo ufw enable`.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe container ports need **no** rules here — Docker's own iptables chains handle the compose bridge[m
[32m+[m[32mnetwork, and nothing is published beyond loopback after step 5.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 7. Bring the stack up[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mcd ~/meal-grocery-assistant[m
[32m+[m[32mdocker compose up -d --build[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mWatch it come up:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mdocker compose ps[m
[32m+[m[32mdocker compose logs -f java-backend      # Ctrl-C to stop following[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStartup ordering is handled in `docker-compose.yml`:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- `postgres` has a `pg_isready` healthcheck; `java-backend` has `depends_on: postgres:[m
[32m+[m[32m  { condition: service_healthy }`, so it won't start until the database accepts connections. On the[m
[32m+[m[32m  **very first** start Postgres also runs every script in `db/init/` against the fresh[m
[32m+[m[32m  `pantry_pg_data` volume — that's what creates the schema and seeds the default rows the app[m
[32m+[m[32m  expects (`user_preference` id 1, `users` id 1). `java-backend` runs Hibernate with[m
[32m+[m[32m  `ddl-auto: validate` and refuses to boot if the schema is missing or stale.[m
[32m+[m[32m- `java-backend` depends on `ml-service` with `condition: service_started` only (not[m
[32m+[m[32m  `service_healthy`) — `MlServiceClient` degrades gracefully to default recipe ordering if[m
[32m+[m[32m  `ml-service` is down, so the backend must not block on it.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mHealth-check from the VM:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mcurl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/inventory        # -> 200, JSON array (possibly [])[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Schema upgrades on an existing volume[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m`db/init/` scripts run **only** against an empty data directory. If you `git pull` a change that[m
[32m+[m[32madds a new script and the `pantry_pg_data` volume already exists, apply it by hand:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mdocker compose exec -T postgres psql -U postgres -d pantrydb < db/init/00X_whatever.sql[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mor, if you can afford to lose the data, recreate the volume: `docker compose down -v && docker compose up -d`.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 8. nginx reverse proxy[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mRun nginx on the **host** (not in a container) so it owns 80/443 directly and certbot can manage it.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32msudo apt-get -y install nginx[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mCreate `/etc/nginx/sites-available/pantry`:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```nginx[m
[32m+[m[32mserver {[m
[32m+[m[32m    listen 80;[m
[32m+[m[32m    listen [::]:80;[m
[32m+[m[32m    server_name pantry.example.com;[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m    # certbot writes ACME challenge files here; everything else -> HTTPS[m
[32m+[m[32m    location /.well-known/acme-challenge/ { root /var/www/html; }[m
[32m+[m[32m    location / { return 301 https://$host$request_uri; }[m
[32m+[m[32m}[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mserver {[m
[32m+[m[32m    listen 443 ssl;[m
[32m+[m[32m    listen [::]:443 ssl;[m
[32m+[m[32m    server_name pantry.example.com;[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m    # placeholder certs until certbot swaps in real ones (step 9)[m
[32m+[m[32m    ssl_certificate     /etc/ssl/certs/ssl-cert-snakeoil.pem;[m
[32m+[m[32m    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/ssl-cert-snakeoil.key;[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m    client_max_body_size 15m;      # receipt / price-tag image uploads[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m    location / {[m
[32m+[m[32m        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;[m
[32m+[m[32m        proxy_set_header Host              $host;[m
[32m+[m[32m        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;[m
[32m+[m[32m        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;[m
[32m+[m[32m        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;[m
[32m+[m[32m        proxy_read_timeout 60s;        # USDA/Gemini lookups on a cold cache can be slow[m
[32m+[m[32m    }[m
[32m+[m[32m}[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mEnable it and reload:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32msudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/pantry /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/pantry[m
[32m+[m[32msudo rm -f /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default[m
[32m+[m[32msudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 9. HTTPS with Let's Encrypt[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32msudo snap install --classic certbot[m
[32m+[m[32msudo ln -sf /snap/bin/certbot /usr/bin/certbot[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32msudo certbot --nginx -d pantry.example.com --redirect --agree-tos -m you@example.com --no-eff-email[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mcertbot edits the nginx config in place to point at the issued certificate and sets up renewal.[m
[32m+[m[32mVerify auto-renewal is scheduled:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32msystemctl list-timers | grep certbot        # snap installs a systemd timer[m
[32m+[m[32msudo certbot renew --dry-run[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mTest from your laptop:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mcurl -fsS https://pantry.example.com/api/inventory[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 10. Day-to-day operations[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m| Task | Command (from `~/meal-grocery-assistant`) |[m
[32m+[m[32m|---|---|[m
[32m+[m[32m| Deploy a new version | `git pull && docker compose up -d --build` |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Tail logs | `docker compose logs -f java-backend ml-service` |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Restart one service | `docker compose restart java-backend` |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Stop everything (keep DB) | `docker compose down` |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Stop and wipe the DB | `docker compose down -v` |[m
[32m+[m[32m| DB shell | `docker compose exec postgres psql -U postgres -d pantrydb` |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Disk usage | `docker system df` — prune old build layers with `docker builder prune` |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Back up the DB | `docker compose exec -T postgres pg_dump -U postgres pantrydb | gzip > backup-$(date +%F).sql.gz` |[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Restore a backup[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mgunzip -c backup-YYYY-MM-DD.sql.gz | docker compose exec -T postgres psql -U postgres -d pantrydb[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 11. Hardening checklist[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- **SSH:** disable password auth (`PasswordAuthentication no` in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`), keep it[m
[32m+[m[32m  key-only. Consider restricting the OCI ingress rule for port 22 to your own IP.[m
[32m+[m[32m- **No unintended public ports:** `sudo ss -ltnp` should show `nginx` on 80/443, `sshd` on 22, and[m
[32m+[m[32m  `docker-proxy` on `127.0.0.1:8080` (loopback). `ml-service` (8000) must not appear at all. The[m
